# LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# There was no .gitattributes, and a Windows checkout defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true — so every text file in the repository arrived with CRLF on
# Windows and LF everywhere else. That is not a cosmetic difference here:
#
#   * the skills shipped in packages/cli/plugin are parsed by their frontmatter
#     delimiter, and `---\r\n` is not `---\n`;
#   * transcript fixtures are JSONL, read line by line;
#   * the published npm tarball and the Docker image are built with LF, so a
#     Windows contributor was building and testing a different artifact from the
#     one that ships.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere, so the
# working tree matches what is published. Binary assets are marked so git never
# touches them.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.zip binary
*.db binary
